---
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wreath from "../components/Wreath.astro";

const entry = await getEntry("resume", "en");
if (!entry) throw new Error("Missing resume content");
const r = entry.data;
const title = "My AI-run personal workflow";
const lead =
  "A self-hosted Obsidian vault that Claude can read and write from any device. AI agents keep my notes, projects, invoices and daily plan in order, and I review what they do.";
---

<Base title={`${title} · ${r.name}`} description={lead} active="ai" nav={r.ui.nav} name={r.name}>
  <main class="article">
    <a class="back" href="/">← {r.ui.nav.resume}</a>
    <header>
      <Wreath alt={r.ui.portraitAlt} />
      <h1>{title}</h1>
    </header>
    <p class="lead">{lead}</p>

    <h2>Why I built it</h2>
    <p>
      I wanted one place for everything: work notes, home maintenance, my band, invoicing for gigs. I also wanted an
      assistant that acts on it, not just chats about it. Most AI tools forget everything between sessions. Giving the
      agent a durable, structured record fixes that.
    </p>

    <h2>How it fits together</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>The vault.</strong> Plain Markdown in Obsidian, organised by the PARA method: Inbox, Projects, Areas,
        Resources, Archive, plus daily notes and a System folder for templates, prompts and skills.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Home server.</strong> A small always-on Linux box runs a headless Obsidian Sync client, so the vault on
        the server, my Mac and my phone stay identical.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>MCP server.</strong> An open-source Model Context Protocol server on the same box exposes the vault as
        tools: search, read, write, edit frontmatter, append to the daily note. It sits behind Tailscale and token auth.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude as the agent.</strong> The server is added as a connector, so any Claude session on phone, desktop
        or in the cloud can work with the vault. Claude Projects give each goal, like this job search, its own space with
        parallel threads and shared memory.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Skills.</strong> Written instructions that teach the agent my conventions: where each kind of note goes,
        how to record a decision, how to issue a Czech invoice with my own CLI and a company lookup in the ARES registry.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Routines.</strong> A scheduled agent writes a morning brief with what is due, new mail and the day's
        calendar. A systemd timer then pushes a short summary to my phone through a self-hosted ntfy server.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What it does for me</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Files and links notes from a one-line message, so the inbox stays near empty.</li>
      <li>Keeps project notes current: status, next step and a dated log after every piece of work.</li>
      <li>Issues and tracks invoices for my gigs and sums income for the tax return.</li>
      <li>Gives me one morning push instead of checking five apps.</li>
      <li>
        Helped build this site: pulled exact job dates from old offer and leaving emails, drafted the copy, ran the design
        discussion and wrote the code that I then reviewed.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Structure beats prompts.</strong> A clear folder scheme and frontmatter do more for agent quality than clever instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>Agents write the record.</strong> Every change ends with a dated note of what was done and why, so the next session starts with context.</li>
      <li><strong>Humans approve the irreversible.</strong> Agents draft emails, invoices and changes freely; anything sent or deleted waits for me.</li>
      <li><strong>Build, then drop what the platform covers.</strong> I wrote a Go + tmux workspace for running parallel coding agents, and retired it when Claude Projects solved the same pains.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I can bring to a team</h2>
    <p>
      The same habits work for an engineering team moving to AI: shared, structured context for agents, conventions
      written down as skills, small automations that remove routine work, and review gates where mistakes are expensive.
      I'm happy to help a team set this up while shipping product work.
    </p>

    <p><a class="back" href="/">← {r.ui.nav.resume}</a></p>
  </main>
</Base>
